1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,935
How big
is Earth's water?

2
00:00:02,115 --> 00:00:04,670
Gather every drop
into one ball,

3
00:00:04,670 --> 00:00:06,215
and measure it.

4
00:00:06,835 --> 00:00:09,750
Count the oceans,
the ice, the lakes,

5
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:11,885
the rivers,
the water underground,

6
00:00:11,955 --> 00:00:13,945
and the water
in the air.

7
00:00:14,085 --> 00:00:17,360
It adds up
to about 1.4 billion

8
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:18,580
cubic kilometers.

9
00:00:19,385 --> 00:00:22,590
Squeeze all of it
into one round ball,

10
00:00:22,595 --> 00:00:25,350
as if it were a single
giant drop.

11
00:00:25,555 --> 00:00:29,520
That ball is about
1,400 kilometers wide.

12
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:33,455
That sounds enormous,
and next to us it is.

13
00:00:33,455 --> 00:00:35,520
But our world
is much bigger,

14
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:38,250
so let's set the ball
next to a planet.

15
00:00:39,185 --> 00:00:42,170
It is less than half
as wide as the Moon.

16
00:00:42,255 --> 00:00:43,580
Next to the Earth,

17
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:46,065
it is a marble
beside a basketball.

18
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,555
Only a small part
of the water is fresh;

19
00:00:50,555 --> 00:00:52,715
count just that,
and the ball

20
00:00:52,715 --> 00:00:55,575
is about 400 kilometers
wide.

21
00:00:56,580 --> 00:00:59,470
Earth looks like a water
world from space.

22
00:00:59,625 --> 00:01:01,820
But its water
is only about

23
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:04,815
an eighth of 1 percent
of its volume.

24
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:06,920
Next envelope:

25
00:01:06,955 --> 00:01:09,375
could we catch all
the world's lightning?
